    "order": 71,
    "notes": "The largest pycnogonid: a tiny, pencil-thin segmented trunk (with proboscis and abdomen about 7 cm) carrying four pairs of very long, thin, jointed legs up to 70 cm across, knees held high above the body like stilts; a long cylindrical proboscis at the front with a pair of slender palps, a small eye tubercle, a stub of an abdomen. Orange-red to crimson. Walks slowly over the slope floor and sucks the tissues of anemones, hydroids and jellies. No bioluminescence.",
